592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090472 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e78377d2-6d96-5995-8daa-2f34a6946e07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090473 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090473 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090473 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a44f84ca-9e0f-579d-a632-df9e6439aae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090474 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090474 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090474 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c1a3e181-9a28-540a-a8b7-4dff46c184fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090475 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090475 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090475 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c615df4c-a176-5a0d-a6b3-6b20bd7589d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090476 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090476 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090476 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dd00d57e-8f74-5b3f-a6d0-53b95fcf8639" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090477 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090477 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186090477 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6dcd1ba6-ce0c-5779-a720-9f227a005c8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091946 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091946 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091946 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091947 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091947 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091947 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091948 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091948 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\nII. $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091948 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092159 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092159 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0f6fda4-4083-5541-8f0e-9a61f740ba8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092159 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186092159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050827 #edb/ref 17592186