ef 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524889 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524889 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524889 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524889 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6c813721-2908-5a92-be17-13c7cb6f3034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524902 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8f34e0d0-1b01-548e-8ace-ce5ffc57a3fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524909 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "97fda8ea-7946-555e-8e3d-7c1f8f5cb679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524929 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524929 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524929 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524929 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce49d847-d598-5aa9-ac7a-bc9bb0b2cb3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524942 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e7b0c143-901f-594e-996e-d3db3b783f1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524952 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524952 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524952 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524952 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524952 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd91f76b-a516-5ea2-bf7f-fa1bf44a8403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524975 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $12$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33175368-8c02-5445-a715-949176f366cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524975 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{3}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $12,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{12}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{3}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{12}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{12}={x}^{3+12}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524980 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n\nII. $P-Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40dff619-4373-5ec6-a963-8c0ddd65024c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524980 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q$ still contains ${x}^{7}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then, $\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}=\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{x}^{7}}=2x,$ which is a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524992 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}$ is a polynomial\n\nII. ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $8$\n\nIII. ${x}^{4}+P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "617b4238-abb7-5b3b-bc81-af12e3c18aa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524992 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}+2.$ Then $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+2},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{4}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{4}={x}^{4+4}={x}^{8},$ meaning that ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=-{x}^{4}.$ Then ${x}^{4}+P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{4}=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524998 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $11$ and $10$, respectively, the degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78e65e3d-79b6-5ea5-b7c8-3423e62535bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524998 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $11,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{11}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $10,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{10}.$\n\nIn $P+Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{11}.$ No ${x}^{11}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{11}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must be $11.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525003 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(3{x}^{4})\\cdot ({x}^{3}-2x)$\n\nII. $(5x)+(-{x}^{7}-8)$\n\nIII. $(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-1)÷({x}^{3})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83c42558-b7ba-54b4-8258-155dbb7534d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525003 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
